two men are due in court in washington  later today accused of impersonating  federal agents and then using financial  favors to get close to the first lady's  security detail more than a dozen fbi  agents raided an apartment in the  nation's capitol yesterday court  documents allege that the suspects  pretended to be  department of homeland security  employees and then paid for pricey  apartments for secret service officials  ed o'keefe is in washington with more on  the story ed not quite sure exactly what  this is but we sure know the optics are  not good for the secret service that is  right gail good morning good to see you  according to the affidavit ariane  taherzadeh and haider ali created a  sophisticated scheme to integrate  themselves with federal law enforcement  court documents show they obtained  police equipment guns and other items  used by federal officers taherzadeh sent  photos of himself and tactical gear to  one secret service agent another photo  shows him with police equipment and a  closet they went as far as using stock  photos to pretend they were in a  training class for homeland security the  two men lived at this d.c apartment  building and falsely told residents they  were federal agents even gaining access  to the building's surveillance cameras  the pair allegedly gave lavish gifts and  paid the rent of some secret service  agents one of them assigned to protect  first lady jill biden and allegedly was  offered a two thousand dollar assault  rifle by tahersaday the plot started  unraveling when a u.s postal inspector  interviewed the two men as potential  witnesses to an unrelated crime just  last month the suspects claimed they  were quote special police and when their  stories didn't match up the fbi was  called in the secret service says all  its personnel involved are on  administrative leave pending an  investigation  so ed let's think about this for a  second we're talking about secret  service agents that had access to the  first lady and then potentially the  president who took gifts and rent money  what else is the secret service saying  about all of this yeah you're right  court documents say more than 40 000 in  rent was paid to at least two agents the  secret service says those involved are  now restricted from accessing agency  facilities equipment and systems but  there's no word on whether further  disciplinary action may be taken  investigators haven't revealed the  motive but according to residents of  that apartment building the pair had  been pretending to be federal law  enforcement for the past couple of  months and what their endgame exactly is  remains unclear hopefully we'll learn  more later this morning  yes yes yes you're on the case more to  come thank you ed
 

